<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Dunoding</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small early kingdom of north-west Wales, named in legend after a son of Cunedda, which became the cantrefi of Eifionydd and Ardudwy in Gwynedd.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to Welsh genealogical tradition, Dunoding was named after Dunod, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunedda">Cunedda</a>, who received the land as his share when Cunedda's family settled north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the 5th century. Historians treat the Cunedda story as a later origin legend, but Dunoding existed as a distinct territory on the coast south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of its rulers or government. The kingdom comprised two districts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifionydd">Eifionydd</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criccieth">Criccieth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardudwy">Ardudwy</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>, a land of coastal farmland backed by mountains, rich in prehistoric monuments. It seems to have been ruled by a local branch of the dynasty of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Dunoding was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd</a> as two cantrefi, probably by the 10th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifionydd">Eifionydd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardudwy">Ardudwy</a> remained administrative districts, later guarded by the castles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criccieth">Criccieth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a> after the English conquest of 1283.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 450 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50